AWS notes (DevOps)

AWS stands for Amazon Web Services.
It is a cloud platform where we can create servers, storage, networks and other infrastructure through the internet instead of buying physical hardware.

Main advantages:

no need to buy servers

pay only for what you use

easy to scale infrastructure

highly available

Regions and Availability Zones

A region is a physical location where AWS has its data centers.

Examples:

us-east-1

ap-south-1 (Mumbai)

Each region has multiple availability zones (AZ).
Availability zones are separate data centers used for high availability.

Example structure:

Region
→ AZ1
→ AZ2
→ AZ3

If one AZ fails, applications can run in another AZ.

EC2

Amazon EC2 is used to create virtual machines (servers) in AWS.

Uses:

hosting applications

running DevOps tools

testing environments

web servers

Important things in EC2

Instance
A virtual server running in AWS.

Instance types
Different hardware sizes.

Examples:
t2.micro
t3.medium
m5.large

Key pair
Used for secure login to EC2.

Example command:

ssh -i key.pem ubuntu@server-ip

Security Groups

Security groups act like a firewall for EC2 instances.

They control inbound and outbound traffic.

Common rules:

Port 22 → SSH access
Port 80 → HTTP (web traffic)
Port 443 → HTTPS

Example:

Inbound rules
SSH (22) → my IP
HTTP (80) → anywhere

S3

Amazon S3 is a storage service used to store files.

It stores data in buckets.

Example structure:

Bucket
→ images
→ backups
→ logs

Uses:

backup storage

static website hosting

storing logs

storing application data

IAM

AWS Identity and Access Management is used to manage permissions in AWS.

It controls who can access AWS resources.

Main components:

User
A person who can log in to AWS.

Group
Collection of users.

Policy
Permissions that allow or deny actions.

Example:
Allow EC2 access
Allow S3 read only

VPC

Amazon Virtual Private Cloud allows us to create a private network in AWS.

Inside a VPC we configure:

subnets
route tables
internet gateway
security groups

Example architecture:

VPC
→ public subnet (web server)
→ private subnet (database)

Public subnet has internet access.
Private subnet does not have direct internet access.

Load Balancer

Elastic Load Balancing distributes traffic across multiple servers.

Example:

Users
↓
Load balancer
↓
Server1
Server2
Server3

Benefits:

high availability

better performance

prevents server overload

Auto Scaling

AWS Auto Scaling automatically increases or decreases the number of servers based on traffic.

Example:

Low traffic → 2 servers
High traffic → 10 servers

Benefits:

saves cost

automatic scaling

improves reliability

CloudWatch

Amazon CloudWatch is used for monitoring AWS resources.

It tracks things like:

CPU usage
memory
disk
network traffic

Example alert:

If CPU > 80%
send notification

Route 53

Amazon Route 53 is a DNS service.

It connects a domain name to an IP address.

Example:

mywebsite.com
↓
EC2 server

